WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.157
Module 17 continues with the other half of the app: the Rust backend.

2
00:00:03.157 --> 00:00:07.000
This episode is a tour of src-tauri/src — one file at a time, one command at a time.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.536
Every one of these files exposes functions marked #[tauri::command].

4
00:00:09.536 --> 00:00:14.756
That attribute is the whole boundary: the frontend never talks to Rust directly, it calls invoke with a command name and arguments, and lib.

5
00:00:14.756 --> 00:00:18.000
rs's generate_handler list is the switchboard that routes it to one of these functions.

6
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:18.184
local.

7
00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:20.766
rs, at just under 800 lines, is the file behind Modules 1, 4, 12 and 22 all at once.

8
00:00:20.766 --> 00:00:22.856
hw_info detects RAM, AVX2 and an NVIDIA GPU for E49's fit indicator.

9
00:00:22.856 --> 00:00:24.362
download and extract_zip pull and unpack a model.

10
00:00:24.362 --> 00:00:26.974
fs_read, fs_write, fs_list and friends are the working-directory file tools from E09.

11
00:00:26.974 --> 00:00:27.988
run_command is the terminal tool.

12
00:00:27.988 --> 00:00:31.000
And start_server, stop_server, server_status supervise the llama-server subprocess from Module 12.

13
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:31.185
mesh.

14
00:00:31.185 --> 00:00:43.000
rs is the largest file in the backend, close to 960 lines, because it's doing the most: mesh_start opens the network listener, mesh_arm_pairing and mesh_pair run the proof-of-knowledge handshake from Module 13, mesh_call sends a request to a paired peer, and mesh_export_peers lets you inspect what's currently trusted.

15
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.814
The rest of the surface, one command group per feature: mcp.

16
00:00:44.814 --> 00:00:46.295
rs connects and calls stdio MCP servers; browser.

17
00:00:46.295 --> 00:00:47.837
rs drives the Chrome extension while inapp_browser.

18
00:00:47.837 --> 00:00:49.530
rs drives the native in-app window from Module 7; audio.

19
00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:50.316
rs is mic capture, speech.

20
00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:51.767
rs is text-to-speech across four engines; oauth.

21
00:00:51.767 --> 00:00:52.765
rs is the MCP OAuth flow; secret.

22
00:00:52.765 --> 00:00:54.458
rs is three tiny functions wrapping the OS keychain; py.

23
00:00:54.458 --> 00:00:56.000
rs runs a Python tool's body and infers its schema.

24
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.358
Notice the pattern across all of it: a command's Rust signature is the actual contract.

25
00:00:59.358 --> 00:01:07.000
fs_read takes a base and a path and returns a String or an error; there's no separate API spec to go stale, because the compiler enforces that the frontend's invoke call matches what's really there.

26
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:13.623
Gotcha worth knowing before you add your own command: everything argument and return type here has to be JSON-serializable — Serialize and Deserialize.

27
00:01:13.623 --> 00:01:17.000
A raw file handle or a thread can't cross this boundary; only plain data can.

28
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:17.739
That's the Rust side.

29
00:01:17.739 --> 00:01:24.000
Now the more unusual half of this app's architecture: how the exact same React tree, calling these exact same invoke names, also runs with zero Rust behind it — in a browser tab.

